# $name

From this directory, install the package and list its operations:

```sh
poetry install
poetry run $name --help
```

Choose a listed operation and run `poetry run $name OPERATION --help`, replacing
`OPERATION` with its actual name. Help describes required JSON inputs and whether
the operation reads or writes data. JSON input names match the generated Python
arguments; the commands call those methods.

Use `--url` for the service base URL or GraphQL endpoint. Add `--login` to open
Chrome, sign in and complete verification codes, then press Enter to call the
endpoint through that browser session. If the sign-in website differs from the
API, supply its address with `--login-url`.

Chrome must be installed and login needs an interactive terminal. Chromium uses
`--browser chromium`; custom executable locations use `--browser-path`. Operation
help explains `--session-header` for additional observed session-header names.
Playwright's Python package includes its Node driver. No separate Node installation
or Playwright browser download is needed because the command uses installed Chrome
or Chromium.

The temporary browser closes after the operation. Each command requires a fresh
sign-in. Ctrl+C cancels. Browser session state is not saved or printed. As an
alternative to browser login, `--headers-stdin` accepts temporary headers as a JSON
object. Do not combine the two authentication methods.

Writes require `--confirm-write` followed by the exact operation name. Requests
are never retried automatically. Use `--output NEW_FILE` for private JSON output.
If a request fails, check the service before retrying because it may have succeeded.

Complex input conversions and custom scalar mappings may require the Python API.
Multi-step service workflows require separate extensions.

